  <text>
    <body>
      <div type="article">
        <head>
          <title type="main">From Railway Station to Museum: The Story of the Musée d’Orsay</title>
          <title type="sub">A Case of Cultural Heritage Transformation</title>
        </head>

        <p>
          The <placeName ref="#gareOrsay">Gare d’Orsay</placeName> was constructed
          in Paris between <date when="1898">1898</date> and
          <date when="1900">1900</date>. Designed mainly by
          <persName ref="#victorLaloux">Victor Laloux</persName>, it opened on
          <date when="1900-05-28">28 May 1900</date> for the
          <rs type="event" ref="#expo1900">Exposition Universelle of 1900</rs>.
          Although it was a modern railway terminal, its monumental exterior followed
          the <term ref="#beauxArtsStyle">Beaux-Arts style</term>.
        </p>

        <p>
          By <date when="1939">1939</date>, the station had become unsuitable for
          longer trains and gradually lost its original function. In the 1970s,
          the former station was preserved and adapted for a new cultural purpose.
          This process is represented as the
          <rs type="event" ref="#orsayTransformation">transformation of the Gare d’Orsay
          into the Musée d’Orsay</rs> and as a case of
          <term ref="#adaptiveReuse">adaptive reuse</term>.
        </p>

        <p>
          The Italian architect and designer
          <persName ref="#gaeAulenti">Gae Aulenti</persName> designed the museum
          interior, adapting the railway hall while retaining visible traces of the
          former station. The building reopened in
          <date when="1986-12">December 1986</date> as the
          <placeName ref="#museeOrsay">Musée d’Orsay</placeName>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Today, the <placeName ref="#museeOrsay">Musée d’Orsay</placeName> is
          especially known for <term ref="#impressionism">Impressionism</term> and
          <term ref="#postImpressionism">Post-Impressionism</term>, including works by
          <persName ref="#claudeMonet">Claude Monet</persName> and
          <persName ref="#paulCezanne">Paul Cézanne</persName>.
        </p>

        <p>
          The history and architecture of the museum are explored in the documentary
          <rs type="documentary" ref="#greatMetamorphosis">Musée d’Orsay - A Great
          Metamorphosis</rs>, produced in France in <date when="2020">2020</date>.
          The museum is also represented online by
          <rs type="digitalResource" ref="#googleOrsayResource">Musée d’Orsay on
          Google Arts &amp; Culture</rs>.
        </p>

        <p>
          The concept of adaptive reuse is documented in
          <rs type="reference" ref="#wikipediaAdaptiveReuse">Adaptive reuse
          (Wikipedia)</rs> and in
          <rs type="reference" ref="#icomAdaptiveReuseArticle">ICOM’s Adaptive Reuse
          reference page</rs>, published by <orgName ref="#icom">ICOM</orgName>.
        </p>

        <p>
          The transformation shows how a historic building can be preserved while
          gaining a new cultural purpose. By retaining visible traces of the former
          railway station, the museum connects its industrial past with its present
          role as a space for art, heritage, and public memory.
        </p>
      </div>
    </body>
